issue.body:
Since cluster mempool (#33629), `BlockAssembler` selects transactions by TxGraph chunks (`CTxMemPool::GetBlockBuilderChunk`, `src/node/miner.cpp`). `node::MiniMiner`, which the wallet uses to calculate the bump fees of unconfirmed inputs, still builds its mock template with the ancestor set scoring algorithm. Its header still describes it as "A minimal version of BlockAssembler, using the same ancestor set scoring algorithm" (`src/node/mini_miner.h`).

The two algorithms disagree on clusters where a low feerate parent is lifted above the target feerate only by several children together. In that case `CalculateBumpFees` and `CalculateTotalBumpFees` can greatly underestimate the fee a CPFP transaction needs, and the result is not monotone in `target_feerate`. In the example below, raising the target from 31.81 to 36.81 sat/vB **lowers** the combined bump fee the wallet pays from 9,950 sat to 4,857 sat. The correct bump fee at 36.81 sat/vB is 11,535 sat.

### Root cause

1. **Ancestor set selection in `BuildMockTemplate` (`src/node/mini_miner.cpp`).** The mock template repeatedly picks the entry with the best ancestor set feerate and stops as soon as that feerate is below `target_feerate`. When a large, low feerate grandparent (`tx0`) has two high feerate children (`tx1`, `tx2`), neither `{tx0, tx1}` nor `{tx0, tx2}` may reach the target while the chunk `{tx0, tx1, tx2}` does. `BuildMockTemplate` then stops without mining anything from the cluster, whereas `BlockAssembler` mines `{tx0, tx1, tx2}` as one chunk.

2. **Bump fees computed over the whole unmined ancestor set.** With nothing mined, both bump fee functions let the surplus fees of `tx1` and `tx2` subsidize the descendants:
   - `CalculateBumpFees` returns `max(target * ancestor_vsize - ancestor_fees, target * vsize - fee)`.
   - `CalculateTotalBumpFees` returns `target * vsize - fees` over every unmined ancestor.

   The real block builder takes `{tx0, tx1, tx2}` first, so that surplus is no longer available to the transactions left behind.

Before cluster mempool, `BlockAssembler` used ancestor set scoring too, so a CPFP paying the MiniMiner bump fee raised its whole ancestor set to the target and was selected. That no longer holds, which makes this a regression from #33629.

### Example

```text
tx0 (1601 vB,     53 sat,   0.03 sat/vB)   parent of tx1 and tx2
tx1 ( 182 vB, 14,985 sat,  82.3  sat/vB)   parent of tx3 and tx4
tx2 ( 311 vB, 68,720 sat, 221.0  sat/vB)   parent of tx3
tx3 ( 180 vB,     79 sat,   0.44 sat/vB)   parent of tx4
tx4 ( 137 vB,     55 sat,   0.40 sat/vB)   queried outpoint tx4:0
```

Package feerates:
- Ancestor set `{tx0, tx2}`: 68,773 sat / 1,912 vB = **35.97 sat/vB**
- Ancestor set `{tx0, tx1}`: 15,038 sat / 1,783 vB = **8.43 sat/vB**
- Chunk `{tx0, tx1, tx2}`: 83,758 sat / 2,094 vB = **40.00 sat/vB**. The mempool's TxGraph linearization makes this the first chunk of the cluster; `tx3` and `tx4` are separate chunks.
- `{tx3, tx4}`: 134 sat / 317 vB = **0.42 sat/vB**

Results from `MiniMiner(pool, {tx4:0})` against a real mempool, measured on master (47187d8163):

| target (sat/kvB) | `CalculateBumpFees` | `CalculateTotalBumpFees` | correct bump fee |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 31,810 | 9,950 | 9,950 | 9,950 |
| 35,000 | 10,961 | 10,961 | 10,961 |
| 35,970 | **4,873** | **2,832** | 11,269 |
| 36,810 | **4,988** | **4,857** | 11,535 |
| 39,990 | **12,524** | **12,524** | 12,543 |

At 31.81 sat/vB, `{tx0, tx2}` (35.97 ≥ 31.81) and then `tx1` are mined, leaving `{tx3, tx4}`. Both functions return `GetFee(317) - 134 = 9,950`, which is correct.

At 36.81 sat/vB, no ancestor set reaches the target, so nothing is mined, even though the chunk `{tx0, tx1, tx2}` pays 40.00 sat/vB:
- `CalculateBumpFees`: `max(GetFee(2411) - 83,892, GetFee(137) - 55) = max(4,857, 4,988) = 4,988`
- `CalculateTotalBumpFees`: `GetFee(2411) - 83,892 = 4,857`

The correct value is `GetFee(317) - 134 = 11,535`: `{tx0, tx1, tx2}` is mined on its own, and `{tx3, tx4}` must reach the target without help.

The underestimate only appears in the target range where the best ancestor set falls short of the target but the chunk does not (here about 35.97 to 40.00 sat/vB).

### Reproduction

The unit test below builds this cluster in a `TestChain100Setup` mempool with exact vsizes, checks that the mempool chunks `{tx0, tx1, tx2}` together, and asserts the expected bump fees:

```text
check individual_bump_fee == expected_bump_fee has failed [4988 != 11535]
check miniminer.CalculateTotalBumpFees(high_target).value() == expected_bump_fee has failed [4857 != 11535]
check individual_bump_fee >= Find(miniminer.CalculateBumpFees(low_target), outpoint) has failed [4988 < 9950]
```

<details>
<summary>Unit test (add to <code>src/test/miniminer_tests.cpp</code>)</summary>

```cpp
/** Build a transaction spending inputs, padding the first input's scriptSig until it has exactly target_vsize. */
static CTransactionRef make_tx_with_vsize(const std::vector<COutPoint>& inputs, size_t num_outputs, int64_t target_vsize)
{
    CMutableTransaction tx{*make_tx(inputs, num_outputs)};
    for (size_t pad{0}; pad < 2000; ++pad) {
        const std::vector<unsigned char> padding(pad, OP_TRUE);
        tx.vin[0].scriptSig = CScript(padding.begin(), padding.end());
        if (GetVirtualTransactionSize(CTransaction{tx}) == target_vsize) return MakeTransactionRef(tx);
    }
    Assert(false);
    return nullptr;
}

BOOST_FIXTURE_TEST_CASE(miniminer_diamond_cluster_chunk, TestChain100Setup)
{
    // Diamond-shaped cluster in which a low feerate grandparent (tx0) is only lifted above the
    // target feerate by both of its children (tx1 and tx2) together:
    //
    //   tx0 (1601 vB, 53 sat)      parent of tx1 and tx2
    //   tx1 (182 vB, 14985 sat)    parent of tx3 and tx4
    //   tx2 (311 vB, 68720 sat)    parent of tx3
    //   tx3 (180 vB, 79 sat)       parent of tx4
    //   tx4 (137 vB, 55 sat)       requested outpoint tx4:0
    //
    //   {tx0, tx2}:      68773 sat / 1912 vB = 35.97 sat/vB
    //   {tx0, tx1}:      15038 sat / 1783 vB =  8.43 sat/vB
    //   {tx0, tx1, tx2}: 83758 sat / 2094 vB = 40.00 sat/vB  (first chunk of the cluster)
    //   {tx3, tx4}:        134 sat /  317 vB =  0.42 sat/vB
    //
    // At a target feerate of 36.81 sat/vB the block builder mines {tx0, tx1, tx2} as its own
    // chunk, so a CPFP of tx4:0 needs to bring {tx3, tx4} to the target on its own.
    CTxMemPool& pool = *Assert(m_node.mempool);
    LOCK2(::cs_main, pool.cs);
    TestMemPoolEntryHelper entry;

    const auto tx0{make_tx_with_vsize({COutPoint{m_coinbase_txns[0]->GetHash(), 0}}, /*num_outputs=*/2, /*target_vsize=*/1601)};
    const auto tx1{make_tx_with_vsize({COutPoint{tx0->GetHash(), 0}}, /*num_outputs=*/2, /*target_vsize=*/182)};
    const auto tx2{make_tx_with_vsize({COutPoint{tx0->GetHash(), 1}}, /*num_outputs=*/1, /*target_vsize=*/311)};
    const auto tx3{make_tx_with_vsize({COutPoint{tx1->GetHash(), 0}, COutPoint{tx2->GetHash(), 0}}, /*num_outputs=*/1, /*target_vsize=*/180)};
    const auto tx4{make_tx_with_vsize({COutPoint{tx1->GetHash(), 1}, COutPoint{tx3->GetHash(), 0}}, /*num_outputs=*/1, /*target_vsize=*/137)};
    TryAddToMempool(pool, entry.Fee(53).FromTx(tx0));
    TryAddToMempool(pool, entry.Fee(14985).FromTx(tx1));
    TryAddToMempool(pool, entry.Fee(68720).FromTx(tx2));
    TryAddToMempool(pool, entry.Fee(79).FromTx(tx3));
    TryAddToMempool(pool, entry.Fee(55).FromTx(tx4));
    BOOST_REQUIRE_EQUAL(pool.size(), 5U);

    // The mempool's cluster linearization puts {tx0, tx1, tx2} in one chunk at 40 sat/vB.
    const FeePerWeight first_chunk{pool.GetMainChunkFeerate(**pool.GetIter(tx0->GetHash()))};
    BOOST_CHECK_EQUAL(first_chunk.fee, 68720 + 14985 + 53);
    BOOST_CHECK_EQUAL(first_chunk.size, (1601 + 182 + 311) * WITNESS_SCALE_FACTOR);
    for (const auto& tx : {tx1, tx2}) {
        BOOST_CHECK(pool.GetMainChunkFeerate(**pool.GetIter(tx->GetHash())) == first_chunk);
    }

    const COutPoint outpoint{tx4->GetHash(), 0};
    const CFeeRate low_target{31810};
    const CFeeRate high_target{36810};
    const int64_t unmined_vsize{180 + 137};
    const CAmount unmined_fees{79 + 55};

    // At the lower target, {tx0, tx2} and then tx1 are mined, leaving {tx3, tx4}.
    {
        node::MiniMiner miniminer(pool, {outpoint});
        BOOST_CHECK_EQUAL(Find(miniminer.CalculateBumpFees(low_target), outpoint), low_target.GetFee(unmined_vsize) - unmined_fees);
    }
    {
        node::MiniMiner miniminer(pool, {outpoint});
        BOOST_CHECK_EQUAL(miniminer.CalculateTotalBumpFees(low_target).value(), low_target.GetFee(unmined_vsize) - unmined_fees);
    }

    // At the higher target, {tx0, tx1, tx2} still meets the target as a chunk, so {tx3, tx4} are
    // the only transactions that need bumping. Expected: 36.81 * 317 - 134 = 11535 sat.
    const CAmount expected_bump_fee{high_target.GetFee(unmined_vsize) - unmined_fees};
    BOOST_CHECK_EQUAL(expected_bump_fee, 11535);
    CAmount individual_bump_fee;
    {
        node::MiniMiner miniminer(pool, {outpoint});
        individual_bump_fee = Find(miniminer.CalculateBumpFees(high_target), outpoint);
        BOOST_CHECK_EQUAL(individual_bump_fee, expected_bump_fee);
    }
    {
        node::MiniMiner miniminer(pool, {outpoint});
        BOOST_CHECK_EQUAL(miniminer.CalculateTotalBumpFees(high_target).value(), expected_bump_fee);
    }

    // A higher target feerate must never require a lower bump fee.
    {
        node::MiniMiner miniminer(pool, {outpoint});
        BOOST_CHECK_GE(individual_bump_fee, Find(miniminer.CalculateBumpFees(low_target), outpoint));
    }
}
```

</details>

